Track 3: Madam Keel’s Nitrate Slipway
- Story & Context: Harbor mythology insists that during the winter solstice hurricane, Madam Keel aligned four synchronized projectors along the drydock seawall to beam a phantom silent epic into the face of a gale, while her sousaphone blew low-end frequencies so loud they flattened the whitecaps. As two hundred ravers stomped across the crunch of shattered flashbulbs and wet ballast stones, the horn began harmonizing with the incoming tide until Keel vanished into the fog, her abandoned instrument still booming out the frantic hook "Sinking in the velvet, spitting out the flare!" via the pneumatic pull of the churning ocean. Harbor pilots still refuse to moor by the slipway at night, claiming the barnacle-covered stones still hum with warm, tape-saturated rave chords whenever the water rises.
